1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,300
A Worker Bee's Life.

2
00:00:02,250 --> 00:00:11,150
A honeybee colony operates as a superorganism, with each individual's function determined not by choice but by developmental timing and chemical signaling.

3
00:00:11,150 --> 00:00:25,687
Within a healthy hive of 30,000 to 60,000 bees during peak summer, a single queen, a few hundred drones, and tens of thousands of sterile female workers form a precisely coordinated society governed by pheromones and age-based labor.

4
00:00:25,687 --> 00:00:32,500
The colony life cycle begins in late winter, when the queen resumes laying eggs after months of reduced activity.

5
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:40,462
She deposits each egg into a hexagonal wax cell, fertilizing eggs destined to become workers and leaving drone eggs unfertilized.

6
00:00:40,462 --> 00:00:50,637
After three days, the egg hatches into a larva, which nurse bees feed with royal jelly for the first two days, then transition to a mixture of pollen and honey called bee bread.

7
00:00:50,637 --> 00:00:55,562
Sixteen days after the egg was laid, a new adult worker emerges.

8
00:00:55,562 --> 00:01:00,687
Worker bees progress through an age-based division of labor called temporal polyethism.

9
00:01:00,687 --> 00:01:10,025
During the first three days of adulthood, workers clean cells and incubate the brood by maintaining the hive at a constant 34.5 degrees Celsius.

10
00:01:10,025 --> 00:01:18,687
From day four to approximately day twelve, they produce royal jelly and brood food from their hypopharyngeal glands and feed developing larvae.

11
00:01:18,687 --> 00:01:31,762
Days twelve through eighteen mark the transition to nest maintenance tasks: receiving nectar from foragers, building comb with wax secreted from abdominal glands, and performing the tremble dance to recruit more nectar processors.

12
00:01:31,762 --> 00:01:40,137
By day eighteen, workers begin guarding the hive entrance, releasing an alarm pheromone, isoamyl acetate, to signal intruders.

13
00:01:40,137 --> 00:01:46,462
At approximately three weeks of age, workers become foragers, the final and most dangerous role.

14
00:01:46,462 --> 00:01:52,787
Foragers make an average of 10 to 15 trips per day, visiting up to 100 flowers per trip.

15
00:01:52,787 --> 00:02:06,525
They communicate the location of rich food sources through the waggle dance, first described by Karl von Frisch in 1946: the angle of the dance relative to the sun indicates direction, while the duration of the waggle run encodes distance.

16
00:02:06,525 --> 00:02:09,662
Seasonal patterns dictate colony fate.

17
00:02:09,662 --> 00:02:13,425
In spring and summer, workers live 4 to 6 weeks.

18
00:02:13,425 --> 00:02:24,087
Autumn-born workers, however, survive 4 to 6 months by reducing activity and forming a winter cluster that consumes stored honey while vibrating flight muscles to generate heat.

